{
  "strategy": "The question is a static probability puzzle likely shown as on-screen text/graphics, so the minimal plan is to retrieve the question-bearing frame(s), read the visible text and diagram details, then only add chart/structure interpretation if OCR alone does not fully reveal the counts or statuses needed to compute the probability.",
  "tool_calls": [
    {
      "step": 1,
      "tool": "frame_retriever",
      "arguments": {
        "video_path": "VIDEO",
        "query": "question screen showing the confusing probability problem with colored circles, percentages, and the multiple-choice options before any transition away from the full problem display",
        "timestamps": null,
        "num_frames": 3
      },
      "purpose": "Get the most relevant static frame(s) containing the full probability question, options, and any visual arrangement of balls/status markers.",
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "step": 2,
      "tool": "ocr",
      "arguments": {
        "frame_path": "<STEP_1:frames>",
        "timestamp": null
      },
      "purpose": "Read all visible on-screen text, including the exact question wording, answer options, percentages, and any labels that define the probability setup.",
      "depends_on": [
        1
      ]
    },
    {
      "step": 3,
      "tool": "chart_analyzer",
      "arguments": {
        "frame_path": "<STEP_1:frames>",
        "timestamp": null,
        "query": "interpret the three-row visual with percentages and colored circles; identify how many circles are shown in each row, what the colors/statuses indicate, and whether the graphic implies counts of defective versus non-defective balls relevant to the probability question"
      },
      "purpose": "If the screen uses structured graphics rather than plain prose, extract the answer-critical mapping from percentages/circles/status indicators to the underlying defective-ball counts.",
      "depends_on": [
        1
      ]
    }
  ],
  "refinement_instructions": "Write a fresh trace from scratch. Use step 2 as authoritative for the exact question text and answer options. Use step 3 only for the structured visual interpretation: how many items/balls are represented, which are defective vs not defective, and what prior information about the pack is actually displayed. Do not invent hidden assumptions beyond what OCR/chart evidence supports. Preserve provenance explicitly, e.g. 'OCR shows...' and 'chart_analyzer indicates...'. The refiner should first restate the observed setup from the screen, then compute the probability of exactly one more defective ball from that observed setup, and finally map the computed value to the listed option. If OCR and chart interpretation disagree or remain partial, state the uncertainty instead of forcing an answer; otherwise update the answer to the option matching the grounded computation."
}
